To resolve this issue, ensure all keys involved in the <code>KeyPair</code> are cryptographically valid and were created using secure mechanisms.<br><br><b>Correct Usage – Generating a KeyPair:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.security.KeyPairGenerator;

KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
keyGen.initialize(4096);
KeyPair keyPair = keyGen.generateKeyPair(); // generatedKeypair[this, "RSA"]
</code></pre><br><b>If Constructing Manually:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.security.KeyPairGenerator;

KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC");
keyGen.initialize(new java.security.spec.ECGenParameterSpec("secp256r1"));
KeyPair generated = keyGen.generateKeyPair();
KeyPair keyPair = new KeyPair(generated.getPublic(), generated.getPrivate()); // publicKey and privateKey are valid
</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b><ul><li>Using raw or deserialized keys that haven't been validated by the crypto provider</li><li>Constructing <code>KeyPair</code> with <code>null</code> or placeholder key objects</li><li>Using <code>KeyPair</code> without prior secure generation</li></ul><br>Ensure that any <code>KeyPair</code> used in cryptographic operations is derived from secure, compliant key generation APIs.

To fix this error, make sure the <code>AlgorithmParameterSpec</code> passed to <code>KeyPairGenerator.initialize(...)</code> is correctly <b>prepared</b> according to the algorithm you are using.<br><br><b>Examples:</b><br><b>For EC:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.spec.ECGenParameterSpec;
import java.security.KeyPairGenerator;

KeyPairGenerator kpg = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC");
kpg.initialize(new ECGenParameterSpec("secp256r1")); // preparedEC
</code></pre><br><b>For RSA:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.spec.RSAKeyGenParameterSpec;
import java.security.KeyPairGenerator;

KeyPairGenerator kpg = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
kpg.initialize(new RSAKeyGenParameterSpec(4096, RSAKeyGenParameterSpec.F4)); // preparedRSA
</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b><ul><li>Passing <code>null</code> or a raw <code>AlgorithmParameterSpec</code> without setting required values</li><li>Using parameter specs that are not appropriate for the algorithm (e.g., using <code>ECGenParameterSpec</code> with <code>RSA</code>)</li></ul><br>Ensuring these predicates are satisfied allows the rule to execute securely and correctly.

To fix this issue, ensure that the <code>PrivateKey</code> instance was generated using a secure and compliant mechanism before being used. <br><br><b>Correct Usage Example – From KeyPairGenerator:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.security.KeyPairGenerator;

KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
keyGen.initialize(4096);
KeyPair pair = keyGen.generateKeyPair();
PrivateKey privateKey = pair.getPrivate(); // generatedPrivkey[this]
byte[] encoded = privateKey.getEncoded();</code></pre><br><b>Correct Usage Example – From KeyFactory:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.KeyFactory;
import java.security.spec.PKCS8EncodedKeySpec;
import java.security.PrivateKey;

byte[] encodedKey = ...; // secure key bytes
PKCS8EncodedKeySpec keySpec = new PKCS8EncodedKeySpec(encodedKey);
KeyFactory keyFactory = KeyFactory.getInstance("RSA");
PrivateKey privateKey = keyFactory.generatePrivate(keySpec); // generatedPrivkey[this]</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b><ul><li>Using hardcoded or static private key values</li><li>Manually constructing a <code>PrivateKey</code> object without a secure generation API</li><li>Using keys that weren’t validated as generated by trusted crypto providers</li></ul><br>Always ensure your <code>PrivateKey</code> has a secure origin to comply with this rule and maintain cryptographic integrity

To fix this issue, ensure that the key material provided to the <code>Signature</code> API has been securely generated and is compatible with the selected algorithm. <br><br><b>Correct Usage for Signing:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.*;

KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
keyGen.initialize(4096);
KeyPair keyPair = keyGen.generateKeyPair();

Signature signature = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
signature.initSign(keyPair.getPrivate()); // generatedPrivkey
signature.update(data);
byte[] signedData = signature.sign();</code></pre><br><b>Correct Usage for Verification:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.*;

KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
keyGen.initialize(4096);
KeyPair keyPair = keyGen.generateKeyPair();

Signature signature = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
signature.initVerify(keyPair.getPublic()); // generatedPubkey
signature.update(data);
boolean valid = signature.verify(signedData);</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b><ul><li>Using raw, hardcoded, or deserialized <code>PrivateKey</code> or <code>PublicKey</code> objects</li><li>Using keys obtained from untrusted or unknown sources without verifying their origin</li><li>Manually constructing keys without proper use of <code>KeyPairGenerator</code> or <code>KeyFactory</code></li></ul><br>Always ensure the keys are generated or validated securely before passing them to <code>initSign()</code> or <code>initVerify()</code> to satisfy the required predicates.
